.\" Manual page for m9c, the M9 compiler.
.\" Checked against m9c --help by runtime/test/mandiff.sh: every option
.\" the program lists must appear here, and every option documented
.\" here must be one the program lists.  A manual page that drifts
.\" from the program is worse than none.
.TH M9C 1 "2026-08-22" "M9" "M9 Compiler Manual"
.
.SH NAME
m9c \- compile an M9 module to C11, and optionally to an object, a program or a library
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B m9c
.RI [ options ]
.IR FILE .m9
.RI [ DEP .m9 ...]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR ccflags ]
.br
.B m9c
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-run
.IR FILE .m9
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B m9c
.BR \-\-help
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B M9
is a Wirth-family language \(em the Modula-2 and Oberon line \(em for
code that is written quickly and audited slowly: data science,
finance, research infrastructure. It aims to read like a bank
statement and to refuse to compile the mistakes those programs
actually make.
.PP
It is designed for code an AI agent writes and a person is answerable
for. A generator is fluent and confident and has no memory of what
went wrong last time, so the rules below are chosen to catch what
fluency misses: a width that silently truncated, a case arm nobody
wrote, a failure never declared, a pointer read without asking. Each
is refused at compile time, so a reviewer reads the intent rather than
hunting for the omission \(em and what the compiler accepts, it has
proved rather than found plausible.
.PP
.IP \(bu 3
Exact-width types only
.RI ( I64 ", " F64 ", " U8
and so on), with no implicit conversion between any two of them.
.IP \(bu 3
Bounds and overflow checks are semantics, not flags. There is no
switch that removes them, and the measured cost is about three per
cent.
.IP \(bu 3
No
.BR NIL .
A pointer that may be absent has type
.B OPT
and cannot be read without
.BR "IS SOME" .
.IP \(bu 3
Every exception a procedure can raise appears in its signature, and
the checker proves the list complete.
.IP \(bu 3
.B CASE
over a variant record must cover every arm.
.IP \(bu 3
Memory is pools and owned pointers, with borrows checked at the
parameter: no collector, and no
.BR free (3)
to get wrong.
.IP \(bu 3
Foreign C is declared, typed, and marked
.B [SERIAL]
or
.BR [REENTRANT] ;
calling a serial one from a thread is a compile error.
.PP
Every one of those rules exists because a real program failed without
it, and the language report \(em which is the normative specification
\(em says which.
.PP
.B m9c
emits C11 and hands it to a C compiler, so an M9 program links against
anything C links against.
.PP
.B m9c
reads an M9 module, checks it, and writes
.IB NAME .h
and
.IB NAME .c
into the working directory, where
.I NAME
is the base name of the source file.
.PP
It checks before it generates. A program with a semantic error
produces diagnostics on standard error, no output files at all, and a
nonzero exit status. A compiler that translates what it knows to be
wrong is a translator.
.PP
One module per invocation, deliberately: the generator holds exactly
one module's registries, and a process is the reset.
.PP
With
.B \-c
or
.BR \-o ,
the generated C is handed to a C compiler and becomes a by-product
rather than the output; see
.BR "THE GENERATED C" .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-I " DIR" "\fR, \fP" \-I DIR
Look for imported modules in
.IR DIR .
May be given more than once; the leftmost is searched first. Both the
separated and the joined spelling are accepted, as
.BR cc (1)
accepts them.
.TP
.B \-c
Compile
.IB NAME .c
to
.IB NAME .o
with the C compiler.
.TP
.BI \-o " EXE"
Compile and link, naming the resulting program
.IR EXE .
.TP
.BI \-\-ar " LIB" .a
Compile, then collect this module and every module in its import
closure into the static library
.IR LIB .a
with
.BR ar (1).
The archive holds M9 modules only: the runtime is its own deliverable
and has its own archive, and a copy of it inside every library is two
.B m9rt
in one link.
.TP
.BI \-\-so " LIB" .so
Compile, then link the same set into the shared object
.IR LIB .so.
A shared object resolves its own symbols at load time, so this one does
get the runtime and the maths library, which an archive does not.
Implies
.BR \-\-pic .
.TP
.B \-\-make
Before compiling, rebuild the stale part of the import closure, in
dependency order \(em deepest first.
.IP
A module is stale when its object is older than its source, and
ALSO when anything it could depend on has just been rebuilt.
The second half is not optional: in
.IR Base " \-> " Mid " \-> " Top ,
changing
.I Base
leaves
.IR Mid 's
object newer than
.IR Mid 's
own source, so a rule that compared each module only against itself
would leave
.I Mid
holding code compiled against the old
.IR Base .
Since the closure is walked deepest first, everything that could
depend on a rebuilt module comes after it, and rebuilding the
remainder is enough. This is deliberately conservative: a later
module that does not in fact depend on the changed one is rebuilt
anyway, which costs a compile and cannot be wrong.
.IP
.B m9c
has always known which modules it needs \(em it resolves, reads and
parses them before checking anything \(em and has always been willing
to NAME the command that builds a missing one. This runs it.
.IP
Each dependency is built by re-invoking
.B m9c
as a subprocess rather than by recursing, because the compiler holds
exactly one module's registries and a process is the reset. The flags
that change what an object IS travel with it \(em the search path,
.BR \-g ,
.B \-\-pic
and anything after
.B \-\-
\(em while those describing the final artifact do not.
.IP
The staleness test is one second's resolution, which is the
resolution the filesystem records. A build that rewrites a source and
recompiles it inside the same second is a build whose inputs nobody
could have read either.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " FILE.m9 \fR[\fIarg\fR ...]"
Build
.I FILE.m9
into the cache and run it with the
.IR arg s;
this process becomes the program, so its standard streams, terminal,
signals and exit status are the program's own, and it is told
.I FILE.m9
as its name (argv[0]). The last option: every word after
.I FILE.m9
is the program's, whatever it looks like. Only
.BR \-I ,
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-no\-unsafe
may come before it.
.IP
Each module of the closure is compiled once, with the optimisation
and inliner budget of the supplied flags but without link-time
optimisation (see SUPPLIED FLAGS), into the cache, keyed by a hash of
its source text and the text of
everything it imports, of this compiler (version and binary), the C
compiler and the runtime; the runtime is compiled once the same way.
A program whose key already has a binary is started straight away,
without being checked again: the same text through the same compiler
has the same verdict, and the binary exists only because that verdict
was yes. A program that changed is checked and compiled alone.
Measured on a tutorial program with a ten-module closure: 5.0 s the
first time, 0.30 s after an edit, 0.02 s unchanged.
.IP
On POSIX systems a file whose first line is
.B #!/usr/bin/m9c \-\-run
is a script; the lexer skips a first line that begins
.BR #! .
On Windows the program is started and waited for, and its status is
returned. Every file reaches its place in the cache by rename, so
concurrent runs are safe. Nothing is ever evicted, and removing the
cache directory is always safe.
.TP
.BI \-\-cell " FILE.m9"
Build
.I FILE.m9
for the notebook session (the Jupyter kernel's in-memory state):
every module of its closure, the runtime and the cell itself as
position-independent shared objects in the cache, and the session host
.IR m9session ,
then print, one line each with fields separated by tabs, what the
session loads: the host, the runtime, each module in dependency order,
and the cell \(em STATE with its module initialiser for a library
module, whose body runs once in the session, or PROGRAM with its main
renamed, run in a fork of the session. Only
.BR \-I ,
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-no\-unsafe
may come before it. Linux and macOS only.
.TP
.B \-\-show
Before
.B \-\-run
or
.BR \-\-cell :
.I FILE.m9
holds an expression, not a module. It is shown by the procedure of the
.B NbShow
module made for its type, which the checker answers: integers, reals
(the shortest decimal that reads back), booleans, characters, strings,
slices of those, a
.BR "GRID 2 OF F64" ,
a frame and a time series; a grid and a frame are also printed as HTML
between two marker lines. Every module the expression names is
imported, and an exception it can raise is reported by name. A type
with no procedure is refused by name. Diagnostics carry the
expression's own lines. The notebook kernel uses it for a cell that
holds only an expression.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " MOD=NAME"
Emit module
.IR MOD 's
C names \(em its procedures, initialiser, exceptions, exported variables
and types \(em under
.I NAME
instead of the module's own name, in this compilation, in every module
it builds for the closure, and in the cache keys of every module whose
closure holds
.IR MOD .
The notebook kernel uses it for a state cell run again: the new
generation and the old live in one process under different names.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-out\-dir " DIR"
Write
.I NAME.h
and
.I NAME.c
into
.I DIR
instead of the working directory. Not with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-ar ,
.B \-\-so
or
.BR \-\-doc .
.TP
.B \-g
Add
.B \-g
to the compiler line, so the object and the program carry debug
information, and emit
.B #line
directives naming
.IB NAME .m9
so that the debug information describes THE M9, not the C it became.
A breakpoint is set on an M9 line, a backtrace names M9 files, and
listing a frame shows M9 source.
.IP
Also, an unhandled exception's message carries the raising
statement's position \(em
.I FILE:LINE:
.B unhandled
.IR NAME \(em
instead of the bare name, because
.B \-g
records the site per statement.  One store per statement, so it stays
out of builds that did not ask for it.
.IP
Implies
.BR \-\-keep\-c .
The debugger no longer needs
.IB NAME .c
\(em the directives point past it \(em but it is what was actually
compiled, and a debug build is the one time it is worth having beside
the program.
.IP
The directives are emitted under
.B \-g
and only under it. Without the flag the generated C is byte for byte
what it has always been, which is what keeps the generator differential
and the bootstrap fixpoint meaningful.
.IP
It ADDS and changes nothing else \(em in particular it does not lower
the optimisation level; see
.B "SUPPLIED FLAGS"
for why, and for what the debug information therefore describes. Be
ready for the consequence: a small procedure is commonly inlined out of
existence, and a debugger will then report it as not defined and its
locals as optimised out. To step through one, take the compiler line
over after
.B \-\-
and ask for an unoptimised build yourself.
.IP
Without this flag m9c emits none at all. Objects are not stripped, so
a backtrace still names
.BR DynStr_Append ;
what is missing is line numbers and locals.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Check the module and its imports, run the generator, print every
diagnostic the build would print \(em the checker's and the
generator's refusals alike \(em and write nothing at all: no C, no
header, no documentation. The exit code says whether the program
exists. This is the flag an editor or a language server wants:
diagnostics without artifacts appearing beside the file being edited.
(Until 0.11 it stopped before the generator, so a construct the
checker accepts and the generator refuses was invisible to editors.)
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-ar ,
.B \-\-so
or
.BR \-\-doc .
.TP
.B \-\-review
Do what
.B \-\-check
does, and if the program is accepted print its review page on standard
output: what the checker PROVED, what a person DECIDED \(em each named
pool, pool parameter, KEPT parameter, use of HEAP, owned allocation,
THREAD and module variable, with the lines they stand on \(em what is
TRUSTED (UNSAFE units and foreign procedures), and how much there was
to check. PROVED says that every function answers on every path, and
then counts the sites where the checker held two types to each other
\(em assignments, arguments, RETURN values, operands, CASE labels,
THREAD arguments \(em and the CASEs over variants it proved total.
The checker never diagnoses a type it could not determine, so each of
those rows also says how many sites it PASSED OVER for that reason,
with the first lines they stand on: a program that compiles has not
had those compared at all. A row whose count is zero is still
printed: an absent line and a zero are different claims. Nothing is
printed for a program the checker or the generator refuses, and
nothing is written. It cannot be combined with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-ar ,
.B \-\-so
or
.BR \-\-doc .
.TP
.B \-\-doc
Check the module, then write
.IB NAME .md
\(em the DEFINITION module as documentation \(em and nothing else. No
C, no object, no linking; it cannot be combined with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-o ,
.B \-\-ar
or
.BR \-\-so ,
and says so rather than guessing which was meant.
.IP
It still CHECKS. Documentation for a program the checker rejects
describes a program that does not exist.
.IP
The document is the module's own comment, then every exported name
with its rendered signature and the comment that follows it. An
undocumented name is LISTED AND MARKED rather than omitted: a
reference that silently drops half an interface is worse than one
that shows the hole.
.IP
A comment may end with a blank line and then a run of
.I "name \-\- what it is"
lines. Every name in one must be a parameter of that procedure; one
that is not is DRIFT rather than a gap \(em the documentation and the
signature have disagreed \(em so it is written into the document and
fails the run. How much is documented is reported under
.B \-v
and is never gated: a threshold on documentation makes an empty
comment a rational move.
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-\-doc ,
which it implies, also write
.IB NAME .json
\(em the same declarations as data. One object: the module, its
comment, and every exported name with its kind, its line and its
comment; for a procedure also the rendered signature, the parameters
as a list of names, mode and type, the result, the RAISES list and
the attribute. Every string is the one the page prints, because the
two are one gather rendered twice. For tools that look a name up
rather than read a page: the one class of mistake a fluent generator
makes most is a real name it did not look up, and a table copied
into a prompt drifts in a day.
.TP
.B \-\-pic
Compile position-independent. Not the default: on x86-64 it costs an
indirection on every global, and the overwhelming majority of these
objects go into a program or an archive, where it buys nothing. It is
usually not needed for
.B \-\-so
either; see
.B "SUPPLIED FLAGS"
for why, and for the one case where it is.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-c
Keep the generated C after
.B \-c
or
.BR \-o .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-unsafe
Refuse a program whose closure declares an
.B UNSAFE
unit or a foreign
.RB ( "FOR \(dqC\(dq" )
unit anywhere outside the trusted library directories \(em
.B $M9LIBRARY
and the installed library \(em naming each refused unit and its
file, before anything is generated.  The shipped library's own
foreign units pass; the point is that untrusted code gets the
checked language and nothing underneath it, so an operating-system
sandbox around the compiled program is a second wall rather than
the only one.
Without this the C is removed once the object exists. The
.I header
is kept by everything except
.BR \-o :
under
.B \-c
it is the module's interface and the next module's generated C
includes it, and for
.B \-\-ar
and
.B \-\-so
it is how anybody calls the library at all.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print the command handed to the C compiler, on standard error, before
running it. This is the only way to see which flags
.B m9c
supplied for itself; see
.BR "SUPPLIED FLAGS" .
A bare
.B \-v
with no input file prints the version instead.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
Pass everything after this point to the C compiler, verbatim \(em and
turn off every flag
.B m9c
would have supplied for itself.
.TP
.BR \-? ", " \-h ", " \-\-help ", " \-\-usage
Print a usage summary and exit successfully. Note that invoking
.B m9c
with no arguments also prints a usage summary but exits with status 1:
asking for help succeeded, getting the usage because you gave no
arguments did not.
.
.SH FINDING MODULES
.B IMPORT
names a module, not a file.
.B m9c
performs that translation itself, so dependencies usually need not be
named on the command line at all.
.PP
The search path is, in order:
.IP \(bu 3
every
.B \-I
directory, leftmost first;
.IP \(bu 3
the directories in
.BR $M9LIBRARY ,
separated as
.B $PATH
is (colons; semicolons on Windows);
.IP \(bu 3
the directory
.IR FILE .m9
itself came from;
.IP \(bu 3
the library beside this compiler,
.IR PREFIX /lib/m9
where
.I PREFIX
is two directories up from the
.B m9c
executable \(em or, when there is none there, the installed
.IR /usr/lib/m9 .
One or the other, never both: a relocated tree does not pick modules
out of the package's library.
.PP
The third entry is why
.B m9c corpus/M9c.m9
needs no
.B \-I
at all: a module's neighbours are almost always its imports. The main
argument is resolved through the path too, so with a library set
.B m9c \-c DynStr
works and the argument may be a module name rather than a path.
.PP
The last entry is what makes a compiler that lives anywhere work with
no variable set: an install under
.I /usr
finds
.IR /usr/lib/m9 ,
a tree
.B build.sh
made under some other
.I DESTDIR
finds its own, and the Windows zip, whose
.I bin/
and
.I lib/m9/
sit under one directory, finds that one.
.B m9c
asks the operating system where its executable is
.RB ( System.Executable ),
not
.IR argv[0] ,
which is whatever the caller typed; where the system cannot say, the
installed locations are all there is.
.PP
A dependency named explicitly on the command line still works and
still wins, because a path someone typed is a statement about which
file they meant \(em and about the order, which is why naming
dependencies does not pull in theirs. Resolution fills only the gaps.
.PP
.B FROM
.I u
.B IMPORT
is deliberately not resolved:
.I u
is a foreign unit backed by C, there is no
.IR u .m9,
and looking for one would invent files. Those are supplied to the C
compiler after
.BR \-\- .
.PP
A module that cannot be found is fatal before generation. Every
missing module is named first, then
.B m9c
stops: without a module's source, everything downstream is guesswork.
.
.SH THE GENERATED C
Without
.B \-c
or
.BR \-o ,
the C is the output and stays. With either, it is a by-product and is
removed once the object exists, unless
.B \-\-keep\-c
says otherwise.
.PP
.B \-c
keeps
.IB NAME .h
even so: it is the module's interface, and the next module's generated
C includes it.
.B \-o
removes both, since nothing downstream is left to include anything.
.PP
The C compiler is
.B $CC
if that is set, and
.B cc
otherwise. It is started directly, with its arguments as a list: no
shell is involved, so nothing is quoted and a file or directory name
may contain a quote, a space or any other character a shell would
read. (Through 0.7.0 the command was composed as one shell line, and a
quote in a name was refused, or, in an include directory, not even
noticed.)
.
.SH SUPPLIED FLAGS
.B m9c
is not a build system, and for a while that was stated by requiring
every include path and every object after
.BR \-\- ,
which made the shortest real command four flags long and three of them
the same every time. Being unwilling to guess is not the same as being
unwilling to look.
.PP
So when nothing follows
.BR \-\- ,
.B m9c
supplies what it can establish by looking:
.IP \(bu 3
.BI \-iquote " RT"
and
.BR "\-iquote ." ,
where
.I RT
is
.B $M9RUNTIME
when that is set and has
.I m9rt.h
in it, else the runtime beside this compiler \(em
.IR PREFIX /runtime
(a source tree, or the zip) or
.IR PREFIX /include/m9
(an install), with
.I PREFIX
as under
.B "FINDING MODULES"
\(em else the installed headers in
.IR /usr/include/m9 ,
else
.I runtime/
or
.I ../runtime/
relative to the working directory;
.IP \(bu 3
.BR \-O2 ,
.B \-flto
and an inliner budget
.RB ( "\-\-param max\-inline\-insns\-auto=200" )
sized for checked code. The compiler's default budget refuses to
inline a body that carries its own bounds and overflow tests, so the
small helpers a hot loop calls stay calls; measured on a real
numerical kernel, raising the budget was worth a factor 2.4 with
results identical to the bit \(em more than removing the checks
outright would have bought. clang accepts and ignores the parameter.
On Windows
.B \-flto\-partition=one
joins them, to keep a link quiet. gcc splits an LTO link into
partitions and compiles them through a
.B make
it expects on PATH; the bundled Windows toolchain ships none, so any
link of two or more partitions falls back to serial compilation and
says so \(em on hello world, every time. One partition has nothing to
parallelise and so nothing to report, and the link was serial anyway;
measured on two real links it costs nothing either way (the compiler's
own came out a second faster and 2% smaller, a small program the same
speed and 1% larger).
.B "This is not a Windows-only wart."
On Linux, where
.B make
is present, plain
.B \-flto
is
.BR \-flto=1 ,
which prints the same warning whenever the partitioner makes two \(em
measured, gcc 15. The quiet and faster spelling there is
.BR \-flto=auto ;
m9c does not yet supply it, and that is owed work rather than a
decision;
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-ffp\-contract=off
(since 0.15.0), so that
.I a * b + c
is rounded twice, as written, on every machine. The C compiler's
default may fuse it into one rounding where the processor has the
instruction (every arm64, not baseline x86\-64), and the same program
then printed other digits on an Apple machine than on a PC. On
baseline x86\-64 the flag changes no instruction.
.IP \(bu 3
and, when linking, one object per module in the import closure it has
just resolved \(em these are not guessed, they are the modules it read
and checked a moment ago \(em together with the runtime, then what the
closure's foreign units
.B LINK
(a library name as
.BR \-l NAME,
a word beginning with
.B \-
as it is, a shim source beside the runtime; NetCDF, Grib, ZarrStore
and Pg name theirs, so a program that imports one links with no flag;
since 0.19.0), then
.B \-Wl,\-\-as\-needed \-lssl \-lcrypto
and
.BR \-lm ,
and on Windows
.B \-lws2_32
in place of OpenSSL, because the runtime's TCP shim is a member on
every platform and its socket calls live in Winsock there rather than
in the C library (an import library costs a program that opens no
socket nothing). OpenSSL is named because the runtime's TLS shim is a
member too (since 0.12; on Windows it is not, the shim being OpenSSL)
and the standard
.B Http
module binds its four symbols from every request path, plain http
included \(em a program that POSTed to a loopback console failed to
link against the 0.11.0 package on exactly those four.
.B \-\-as\-needed
keeps both libraries out of a binary that referenced nothing in them.
.PP
The runtime that is linked is the one whose header was included: the
sources beside that header when they are there, and an archive only
when they are not \(em
.IR PREFIX /lib/libm9rt.a
beside this compiler (linked as
.B \-L
.I PREFIX/lib
.BR \-lm9rt ),
else the installed
.IR /usr/lib/libm9rt.a .
An installed archive underneath a source tree's header is how a newly
added runtime function becomes an undefined reference at link time
rather than a missing declaration at compile time; through 0.7.0 a
compiler built in a source tree and run with no variable set did
exactly that, because the defaults were the install locations spelled
as constants rather than places relative to the executable.
.PP
A missing object is named, with the command that makes it, because the
alternative is the C linker reporting our omission in its own
vocabulary. Nothing is compiled in that case.
.PP
Anything after
.B \-\-
turns all of this off: the caller has taken the command line over, and
a default added silently underneath a flag someone chose is worse than
no default at all. Use
.B \-v
to see what was supplied.
.PP
.B "What \-g describes."
.B \-g
adds debug information and changes nothing else. It deliberately does
not lower the optimisation level: silently altering how a program is
compiled under a flag that asks for debug information is
flag-dependent semantics, which is exactly what this language refuses
by name. The defaults above are kept, so what a debugger reads are
optimised-code line tables \(em accurate about which statement, and
free to have moved or merged one. For an unoptimised build, take the
line over with
.BR \-\- .
.PP
Because the defaults include link-time optimisation, an object
compiled with it holds intermediate code rather than machine code, and
the debug information is produced when the program is LINKED \(em not
in the object file, where looking for it finds nothing. This is the
same mechanism described under position independence above.
.PP
The information describes the GENERATED C, because that is what the C
compiler was given:
.I DW_AT_name
is
.IB NAME .c
and not
.IB NAME .m9 .
That is why
.B \-g
implies
.BR \-\-keep\-c .
Mapping the C back to M9 source would need line directives from the
generator, which would move every byte it emits; it is not done here.
.PP
It keeps THIS module's C, and only this module's. A dependency's C
was removed by the run that compiled it, so stepping into one needs
that module to have been built with
.B \-g
as well \(em otherwise a debugger stops in the right function and
reports
.I "No such file or directory"
for the line. One module per invocation is the rule everywhere else
in this program, and it applies here too.
.PP
.B "Position independence, and why \-\-pic is usually unnecessary."
Building a shared object with
.B \-\-so
would seem to require every member to be compiled
.BR \-fPIC .
Measured, that is only half true. Because
.B m9c
compiles with
.BR \-flto ,
an object produced by
.B \-c
holds intermediate code rather than machine code, and the code
generation happens at
.I link
time \(em where
.B \-shared
asks for position independence and gets it. A closure built with a
plain
.B "m9c \-c"
therefore goes into a shared object and works.
.PP
Put anything after
.B \-\-
and LTO departs with the rest of the defaults. The object is then
really machine code, and
.BR ld (1)
refuses it:
.IP
.I "relocation R_X86_64_PC32 against `m9_exc_IndexError'"
.br
.I "can not be used when making a shared object; recompile with \-fPIC"
.PP
That is the case
.B \-\-pic
exists for, and
.B m9c
names it when the link fails. One further trap, also measured: the
runtime arrives as
.IR sources ,
so the shared link compiles
.I m9rt.c
on the way through, and
.BR gcc (1)
does not imply
.B \-fPIC
from
.BR \-shared .
.B m9c
supplies it there itself.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B M9CACHE
The cache
.B \-\-run
builds into. Unset, it is
.I m9
under
.B $XDG_CACHE_HOME
or
.I ~/.cache/m9
(on Windows, under
.BR %LOCALAPPDATA% ).
.TP
.B M9LIBRARY
List of directories to search for imported modules, after any
.B \-I
directories, separated as
.B $PATH
is on the platform: colons, or semicolons on Windows.
.TP
.B M9RUNTIME
The directory holding
.IR m9rt.h ,
searched before the runtime beside the compiler and the installed
headers when
.B m9c
supplies the include paths itself. If it names a directory with no
.I m9rt.h
in it,
.B m9c
says so and keeps looking: a variable that is set is not thereby
right.
.TP
.B CC
The C compiler to invoke for
.B \-c
and
.BR \-o .
Defaults to
.BR cc ;
on Windows, to the
.B gcc
bundled beside the compiler
.RI ( PREFIX /ucrt64/bin/gcc.exe)
when there is one, and the archiver for
.B \-\-ar
is found the same way.
This is the compiler's
.IR name ,
a word looked up on the PATH or a path to the executable, not a
command line: there is no shell to split it, so
.B CC='gcc \-m32'
would be looked up as one program and not found. Flags for the
compiler go after
.BR \-\- .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The module was checked, generated, and compiled if asked.
.TP
.B 1
Something failed: a file could not be read, an import could not be
found, the parser or the checker rejected the program, the generator
refused a construct, or the C compiler reported errors. All
diagnostics go to standard error.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate C for a module, then compile it by hand:
.PP
.RS
.EX
m9c corpus/Json.m9
cc \-c \-iquote runtime \-iquote . Json.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same, in one step, from the top of a source tree \(em the include
paths are found rather than typed:
.PP
.RS
.EX
m9c \-c corpus/Json.m9
.EE
.RE
.PP
A whole program, with no make and no build script. Nothing here names
a path, an object, or the runtime:
.PP
.RS
.EX
export M9LIBRARY=corpus
m9c \-c DynStr
m9c \-c Io
m9c \-o hello Hello
.EE
.RE
.PP
Installed, not even
.B $M9LIBRARY
is needed, since
.I /usr/lib/m9
is on the search path already:
.PP
.RS
.EX
m9c \-c DynStr
m9c \-c Io
m9c \-o hello hello.m9
.EE
.RE
.PP
And when one of those defaults is wrong, take the command line over.
Everything after
.B \-\-
is the whole of it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
m9c \-c corpus/Json.m9 \-\- \-O2 \-iquote runtime \-iquote .
.EE
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
Checks are semantics in M9, not flags. Bounds and overflow checking
are part of what a program means and cannot be switched off; the
measured cost is a few per cent \(em provided the inliner budget
.B m9c
supplies (see
.BR "SUPPLIED FLAGS" ):
under the compiler's default budget the checks' real price was the
inlining they suppressed, not the tests themselves.
.
